::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has side lengths 5, 5, and 8. How is it classified by sides? {
=A. Isosceles#Correct. Two side lengths are equal, so the triangle is isosceles.
~B. Scalene#Scalene means no equal sides, but this triangle has two sides of length 5.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral means all three sides are equal, but only two side lengths match.
~D. Right#Right classifies by angle measure, not by side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has side lengths 4, 6, and 9. How is it classified by sides? {
~A. Isosceles#Isosceles requires at least two congruent sides, which are not shown.
=B. Scalene#Correct. All three side lengths are different, so the triangle is scalene.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral requires three congruent sides.
~D. Obtuse#Obtuse classifies by angle measure, not side length.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 35&deg;, 55&deg;, and 90&deg;. How is it classified by angles? {
~A. Acute#Acute means all angles are less than 90&deg;, but one angle is exactly 90&deg;.
~B. Obtuse#Obtuse means one angle is greater than 90&deg;.
=C. Right#Correct. A triangle with one 90&deg; angle is a right triangle.
~D. Equilateral#Equilateral classifies by sides, and equilateral triangles have three 60&deg; angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 40&deg;, 65&deg;, and 75&deg;. How is it classified by angles? {
~A. Right#A right triangle must have one 90&deg; angle.
~B. Obtuse#An obtuse triangle must have one angle greater than 90&deg;.
~C. Scalene#Scalene classifies by side lengths, not angle measures.
=D. Acute#Correct. All three angles are less than 90&deg;, so the triangle is acute.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has angles 25&deg;, 35&deg;, and 120&deg;. How is it classified by angles? {
=A. Obtuse#Correct. The 120&deg; angle is greater than 90&deg;, so the triangle is obtuse.
~B. Acute#Acute requires all angles to be less than 90&deg;.
~C. Right#Right requires one angle to be exactly 90&deg;.
~D. Isosceles#Isosceles classifies by side lengths, not angle measures.
}

